1
00:00:01,980 --> 00:00:04,200
The weapons wouldn't be the first problem.

2
00:00:04,250 --> 00:00:06,270
The first problem would be the silence.

3
00:00:06,320 --> 00:00:09,000
No birdsong. No grass.

4
00:00:09,050 --> 00:00:13,040
No flowers. Just wind moving through ferns, the smell of

5
00:00:13,090 --> 00:00:15,600
resin and rotting wood, and somewhere in the

6
00:00:15,650 --> 00:00:18,760
distance a sound no living person has ever heard.

7
00:00:18,810 --> 00:00:21,250
A low, rumbling call from an

8
00:00:21,300 --> 00:00:23,820
animal heavier than a loaded truck.

9
00:00:23,870 --> 00:00:26,080
Now imagine one hundred modern soldiers

10
00:00:26,130 --> 00:00:31,580
standing in the middle of that. They have rifles, body armor, radios, and training.

11
00:00:31,630 --> 00:00:34,630
They have one mission. Survive for seven days.

12
00:00:34,680 --> 00:00:38,810
They are about 150 million years in the past.

13
00:00:38,860 --> 00:00:40,920
And everything around them has spent millions

14
00:00:40,970 --> 00:00:44,070
of years evolving without ever seeing a human.

15
00:00:44,120 --> 00:00:47,680
The place is western North America during the Late Jurassic.

16
00:00:47,730 --> 00:00:51,270
Today, the rocks from this time are called the Morrison Formation.

17
00:00:51,320 --> 00:00:54,730
They stretch across Colorado, Utah, Wyoming, and Montana.

18
00:00:54,780 --> 00:00:58,760
They are one of the richest dinosaur graveyards ever found.

19
00:00:58,810 --> 00:01:01,150
That matters, because it means we know a surprising

20
00:01:01,200 --> 00:01:03,610
amount about where our soldiers would land.

21
00:01:03,660 --> 00:01:06,980
We know which predators lived there. We know which plants grew there.

22
00:01:07,030 --> 00:01:10,120
We even know some of the injuries those animals suffered.

23
00:01:10,170 --> 00:01:13,200
So the question isn't just whether a hundred soldiers could survive.

24
00:01:13,250 --> 00:01:16,650
It's which part of this world would kill them first.

25
00:01:16,700 --> 00:01:19,360
The first hours would feel strangely calm.

26
00:01:19,410 --> 00:01:22,660
The landscape would look half familiar and half wrong.

27
00:01:22,710 --> 00:01:27,050
There would be wide floodplains, slow rivers, and forests of conifers.

28
00:01:27,100 --> 00:01:30,990
There would be tree ferns, cycads with thick trunks, ginkgo trees,

29
00:01:31,040 --> 00:01:33,960
and huge stands of horsetails along the water.

30
00:01:34,010 --> 00:01:36,190
But something important would be missing.

31
00:01:36,240 --> 00:01:38,550
There would be no grass anywhere.

32
00:01:38,600 --> 00:01:40,440
Grasses hadn't evolved yet.

33
00:01:40,490 --> 00:01:42,700
There would be no flowering plants either.

34
00:01:42,750 --> 00:01:44,610
Every bit of green the soldiers could see

35
00:01:44,660 --> 00:01:49,150
would come from older groups of plants. And the air itself would be different.

36
00:01:49,200 --> 00:01:51,390
Scientists believe carbon dioxide levels in the

37
00:01:51,440 --> 00:01:54,360
Jurassic were several times higher than today.

38
00:01:54,410 --> 00:01:56,490
Oxygen levels are harder to pin down.

39
00:01:56,540 --> 00:01:58,670
Some models suggest slightly less than now,

40
00:01:58,720 --> 00:02:01,380
others suggest more. Either way, the air would

41
00:02:01,430 --> 00:02:03,240
almost certainly be breathable.

42
00:02:03,290 --> 00:02:07,440
But high carbon dioxide could still make some soldiers feel heavy and tired.

43
00:02:07,490 --> 00:02:09,450
And the weather would be hot.

44
00:02:09,500 --> 00:02:14,610
The Morrison region was likely semi-arid, with long dry seasons broken by wet ones.

45
00:02:14,660 --> 00:02:17,080
That makes the first real enemy obvious.

46
00:02:17,130 --> 00:02:20,450
Water. A hundred soldiers need a lot of it.

47
00:02:20,500 --> 00:02:22,410
In heat like this, each person could need

48
00:02:22,460 --> 00:02:24,510
several liters a day just to function.

49
00:02:24,560 --> 00:02:26,920
That's hundreds of liters for the group,

50
00:02:26,970 --> 00:02:29,970
every single day. The rivers would provide it.

51
00:02:30,020 --> 00:02:33,770
Water purification tablets and filters could make it safer.

52
00:02:33,820 --> 00:02:37,370
But every animal in the region would need that same water.

53
00:02:37,420 --> 00:02:40,680
And the rivers were exactly where the predators waited.

54
00:02:40,730 --> 00:02:42,860
Food would be the second problem.

55
00:02:42,910 --> 00:02:45,720
The soldiers might carry a few days of rations.

56
00:02:45,770 --> 00:02:48,440
After that, they would need to find something to eat.

57
00:02:48,490 --> 00:02:52,330
And this is where the Jurassic becomes quietly dangerous.

58
00:02:52,380 --> 00:02:56,070
Many of the plants around them would be poisonous or close to useless.

59
00:02:56,120 --> 00:02:58,410
Cycads contain toxins that can

60
00:02:58,460 --> 00:03:00,770
damage the liver and nervous system.

61
00:03:00,820 --> 00:03:03,210
Some ferns and horsetails contain a compound

62
00:03:03,260 --> 00:03:05,500
that destroys vitamin B1 in the body.

63
00:03:05,550 --> 00:03:09,470
Eat enough of them, and your nerves and muscles start to fail.

64
00:03:09,520 --> 00:03:11,660
So the safest food wouldn't be plants.

65
00:03:11,710 --> 00:03:15,723
It would be animals. The rivers held fish, including lungfish.

66
00:03:15,773 --> 00:03:18,620
There were also small mammals, lizards,

67
00:03:18,670 --> 00:03:21,030
and early relatives of crocodiles.

68
00:03:21,080 --> 00:03:23,610
Hunting small animals would be possible.

69
00:03:23,660 --> 00:03:26,080
But to feed a hundred people, the soldiers

70
00:03:26,130 --> 00:03:28,400
might start thinking about something bigger.

71
00:03:28,450 --> 00:03:32,340
And in the Morrison Formation, there was plenty of something bigger.

72
00:03:32,390 --> 00:03:35,990
On the second day, they would almost certainly see them.

73
00:03:36,410 --> 00:03:41,230
Sauropods. Diplodocus, Apatosaurus, Brachiosaurus.

74
00:03:41,280 --> 00:03:43,170
Animals with necks longer than a school

75
00:03:43,220 --> 00:03:47,340
bus and bodies that could weigh as much as several elephants combined.

76
00:03:47,390 --> 00:03:51,110
Some could stretch more than twenty-five meters from head to tail.

77
00:03:51,160 --> 00:03:54,090
To the soldiers, these giants might look peaceful.

78
00:03:54,140 --> 00:03:58,340
They would be wrong. A sauropod didn't need to attack a human to kill one.

79
00:03:58,390 --> 00:04:01,210
It only needed to step in the wrong place.

80
00:04:01,260 --> 00:04:05,340
And some scientists think their tails could be used as defensive weapons.

81
00:04:05,390 --> 00:04:07,690
In the 1990s, researchers suggested a Diplodocus

82
00:04:07,740 --> 00:04:10,790
tail tip might even break the sound barrier.

83
00:04:10,840 --> 00:04:12,900
Later studies questioned that idea.

84
00:04:12,950 --> 00:04:15,600
A 2022 computer model suggested the tail

85
00:04:15,650 --> 00:04:19,050
moved fast, but probably not supersonic.

86
00:04:19,100 --> 00:04:21,900
Still, a whip of muscle and bone that long,

87
00:04:21,950 --> 00:04:26,240
moving that fast, would not be something anyone wants to stand behind.

88
00:04:26,290 --> 00:04:28,600
But the sauropods weren't the real threat.

89
00:04:28,650 --> 00:04:31,750
The real threat was the animal that hunted them.

90
00:04:31,800 --> 00:04:35,200
Imagine the soldiers set up camp near the river on the third night.

91
00:04:35,250 --> 00:04:37,160
They dig in. They post sentries.

92
00:04:37,210 --> 00:04:41,860
They use night vision. And then something walks out of the trees.

93
00:04:41,910 --> 00:04:45,250
Allosaurus. Around eight to nine meters long.

94
00:04:45,300 --> 00:04:47,890
Long legs, a big skull, and curved,

95
00:04:47,940 --> 00:04:50,820
serrated teeth built to slice flesh.

96
00:04:50,870 --> 00:04:54,250
It was the most common large predator of the Morrison Formation.

97
00:04:54,300 --> 00:04:58,940
And we know just how common it was because of one strange place in Utah.

98
00:04:58,990 --> 00:05:01,040
The Cleveland-Lloyd Dinosaur Quarry.

99
00:05:01,090 --> 00:05:03,640
When paleontologists began digging there,

100
00:05:03,690 --> 00:05:06,300
they found bones. Thousands of them.

101
00:05:06,350 --> 00:05:11,400
That alone wasn't unusual. What was unusual was who those bones belonged to.

102
00:05:11,450 --> 00:05:13,760
Most of them came from predators.

103
00:05:13,810 --> 00:05:17,840
Researchers have identified the remains of dozens of Allosaurus at the site.

104
00:05:17,890 --> 00:05:20,310
At least forty-six individuals, of different ages,

105
00:05:20,360 --> 00:05:23,540
all packed together in one place.

106
00:05:23,590 --> 00:05:27,540
In most fossil sites, plant-eaters vastly outnumber meat-eaters.

107
00:05:27,590 --> 00:05:29,710
Here, the pattern was flipped.

108
00:05:29,760 --> 00:05:32,760
What happened? Scientists still don't fully agree.

109
00:05:32,810 --> 00:05:35,290
Some think the animals were trapped in mud.

110
00:05:35,340 --> 00:05:37,420
Some think they gathered around shrinking water

111
00:05:37,470 --> 00:05:39,500
during a drought and died there over time.

112
00:05:39,550 --> 00:05:42,770
Others suspect the bones were collected by floods.

113
00:05:42,820 --> 00:05:47,050
We still don't know for sure. But for our soldiers, the lesson is simple.

114
00:05:47,100 --> 00:05:48,900
Allosaurus wasn't rare.

115
00:05:48,950 --> 00:05:52,270
And it may have been drawn to the same water they depended on.

116
00:05:52,320 --> 00:05:54,760
So how would modern weapons do against it?

117
00:05:54,810 --> 00:05:56,680
This is where things get uncomfortable.

118
00:05:56,730 --> 00:06:00,680
A standard infantry rifle fires a small, fast bullet.

119
00:06:00,730 --> 00:06:04,230
It's designed to stop people, not multi-ton animals.

120
00:06:04,280 --> 00:06:07,910
Hunters usually choose much larger calibers just for elk and bear.

121
00:06:07,960 --> 00:06:11,850
A single rifle shot would probably not stop an Allosaurus.

122
00:06:11,900 --> 00:06:15,570
But one hundred trained soldiers don't fire single shots.

123
00:06:15,620 --> 00:06:18,540
Concentrated fire from many rifles, especially with

124
00:06:18,590 --> 00:06:22,200
machine guns in the mix, could bring down even a large predator,

125
00:06:22,250 --> 00:06:25,110
particularly with hits to the head or chest.

126
00:06:25,160 --> 00:06:27,420
A company that stayed together, kept watch,

127
00:06:27,470 --> 00:06:30,430
and reacted fast would have a real chance.

128
00:06:30,480 --> 00:06:33,920
The danger is everything that comes before the shooting.

129
00:06:33,970 --> 00:06:36,320
Allosaurus may have relied on ambush.

130
00:06:36,370 --> 00:06:38,300
Some researchers think it attacked with its upper

131
00:06:38,350 --> 00:06:41,220
jaw like a hatchet, using quick slashing bites.

132
00:06:41,270 --> 00:06:43,430
A soldier on watch at the tree line

133
00:06:43,480 --> 00:06:45,920
might get only a few seconds of warning.

134
00:06:45,970 --> 00:06:48,860
And we have proof of how tough these animals were.

135
00:06:48,910 --> 00:06:51,330
In 1991, a team in Wyoming uncovered a

136
00:06:51,380 --> 00:06:54,050
remarkably complete Allosaurus skeleton.

137
00:06:54,100 --> 00:06:56,600
They nicknamed it Big Al.

138
00:06:56,650 --> 00:06:59,470
At first, it looked like a beautiful specimen.

139
00:06:59,520 --> 00:07:02,010
Then they looked closer at the bones.

140
00:07:02,060 --> 00:07:04,640
Big Al was covered in injuries.

141
00:07:04,690 --> 00:07:06,840
Broken ribs. Damaged vertebrae.

142
00:07:06,890 --> 00:07:09,050
Infected bones in the foot.

143
00:07:09,100 --> 00:07:11,090
Scientists have counted around nineteen

144
00:07:11,140 --> 00:07:13,300
separate injuries or infections.

145
00:07:13,350 --> 00:07:15,190
Some of those wounds had healed.

146
00:07:15,240 --> 00:07:17,800
This animal kept living and hunting while carrying

147
00:07:17,850 --> 00:07:20,360
damage that would have stopped most creatures.

148
00:07:20,410 --> 00:07:23,430
Which raises a terrifying possibility for the soldiers.

149
00:07:23,480 --> 00:07:26,810
A wounded Allosaurus might not run away.

150
00:07:26,860 --> 00:07:29,850
And Allosaurus wasn't the only predator out there.

151
00:07:29,900 --> 00:07:33,670
The Morrison Formation was also home to Ceratosaurus, a predator with a

152
00:07:33,720 --> 00:07:36,430
horn on its nose and blade-like teeth.

153
00:07:36,480 --> 00:07:40,190
And there was Torvosaurus, which may have been even bigger than Allosaurus,

154
00:07:40,240 --> 00:07:43,180
with a massive skull and powerful arms.

155
00:07:43,230 --> 00:07:45,950
There were smaller hunters too, like Ornitholestes,

156
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:48,060
roughly the size of a large dog.

157
00:07:48,110 --> 00:07:50,930
But if the soldiers want to know what a dangerous plant-eater looks like,

158
00:07:50,980 --> 00:07:53,435
they only need to meet Stegosaurus.

159
00:07:53,485 --> 00:07:57,040
At first, it might seem slow and harmless.

160
00:07:57,090 --> 00:08:00,950
Big plates along its back. A small head close to the ground.

161
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:05,950
Then it turns. At the end of its tail are four long spikes.

162
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:08,160
For years, scientists debated whether those

163
00:08:08,210 --> 00:08:11,350
spikes were really weapons or just for display.

164
00:08:11,400 --> 00:08:13,580
Then researchers examined an Allosaurus

165
00:08:13,630 --> 00:08:15,590
tail bone with a strange wound in it.

166
00:08:15,640 --> 00:08:18,880
A hole had been punched straight through the bone.

167
00:08:18,930 --> 00:08:21,060
When they compared it to a Stegosaurus spike,

168
00:08:21,110 --> 00:08:24,440
the shape matched. The wound showed signs of infection,

169
00:08:24,490 --> 00:08:27,850
suggesting the Allosaurus lived for some time afterward.

170
00:08:27,900 --> 00:08:31,640
But the message was clear. Stegosaurus fought back.

171
00:08:31,690 --> 00:08:33,870
And it hit hard enough to pierce bone.

172
00:08:33,920 --> 00:08:35,770
If an animal could do that to a predator

173
00:08:35,820 --> 00:08:38,440
built for combat, a soldier in body armor

174
00:08:38,490 --> 00:08:40,840
wouldn't stand a chance at close range.

175
00:08:40,890 --> 00:08:42,710
By day five, the real problem

176
00:08:42,760 --> 00:08:46,190
wouldn't be any single animal. It would be exhaustion.

177
00:08:46,240 --> 00:08:49,200
Ammunition is limited. A standard combat load for a

178
00:08:49,250 --> 00:08:51,520
soldier is a few hundred rounds.

179
00:08:51,570 --> 00:08:53,780
Every encounter would burn through it.

180
00:08:53,830 --> 00:08:56,450
Every night on watch would wear people down.

181
00:08:56,500 --> 00:08:58,980
Every trip to the river would be a risk.

182
00:08:59,030 --> 00:09:02,039
And then there's the smallest threat of all.

183
00:09:02,320 --> 00:09:06,150
Infection. Bacteria have existed for billions of years.

184
00:09:06,200 --> 00:09:08,550
A cut from a tooth, a claw,

185
00:09:08,600 --> 00:09:10,950
or even a sharp branch could become infected.

186
00:09:11,000 --> 00:09:13,340
And with no hospital, even a minor

187
00:09:13,390 --> 00:09:16,030
wound could turn serious within days.

188
00:09:16,080 --> 00:09:19,010
No one knows what kinds of microbes lived in the Jurassic,

189
00:09:19,060 --> 00:09:21,590
or how a human body would react to them.

190
00:09:21,640 --> 00:09:23,930
But here is where the story flips.

191
00:09:23,980 --> 00:09:26,820
The soldiers wouldn't just be at risk from this world.

192
00:09:26,870 --> 00:09:29,600
This world might be at risk from them.

193
00:09:29,650 --> 00:09:32,380
Every human carries trillions of bacteria.

194
00:09:32,430 --> 00:09:35,270
Some live on the skin. Others live in the gut.

195
00:09:35,320 --> 00:09:38,300
These microbes evolved alongside mammals over tens

196
00:09:38,350 --> 00:09:41,010
of millions of years that hadn't happened yet.

197
00:09:41,060 --> 00:09:43,180
What would happen if those microbes spread into a

198
00:09:43,230 --> 00:09:46,300
Jurassic ecosystem that had never encountered them?

199
00:09:46,350 --> 00:09:49,730
We simply don't know. Most would probably die off.

200
00:09:49,780 --> 00:09:53,160
Some might do nothing at all. But it's a strange thought.

201
00:09:53,210 --> 00:09:55,090
The most dangerous thing the soldiers brought

202
00:09:55,140 --> 00:09:57,680
back in time might not be their rifles.

203
00:09:57,730 --> 00:09:59,860
It might be invisible.

204
00:09:59,910 --> 00:10:02,030
So could one hundred modern soldiers

205
00:10:02,080 --> 00:10:04,270
survive one week in the Jurassic?

206
00:10:04,320 --> 00:10:08,040
Most of them probably could. If they stayed together, stayed away from the

207
00:10:08,090 --> 00:10:10,460
river at night, avoided the sauropods,

208
00:10:10,510 --> 00:10:12,700
didn't eat the wrong plants, and treated every

209
00:10:12,750 --> 00:10:14,410
shadow at the tree line as a threat,

210
00:10:14,460 --> 00:10:17,940
a well-trained group would likely make it seven days.

211
00:10:17,990 --> 00:10:22,560
But not without losses. And not without learning something deeply humbling.

212
00:10:22,610 --> 00:10:25,020
On day seven, as the soldiers wait to leave,

213
00:10:25,070 --> 00:10:27,360
they might look out across the floodplain.

214
00:10:27,410 --> 00:10:29,590
Sauropods moving like slow hills.

215
00:10:29,640 --> 00:10:32,330
Stegosaurus grazing by the water.

216
00:10:32,380 --> 00:10:35,490
Somewhere in the trees, an Allosaurus watching.

217
00:10:35,540 --> 00:10:38,090
None of these animals know what a human is.

218
00:10:38,140 --> 00:10:40,470
None of them know that one day, their bones will be

219
00:10:40,520 --> 00:10:43,240
pulled from the ground in Utah and Wyoming and

220
00:10:43,290 --> 00:10:46,410
studied by the very species standing in front of them.

221
00:10:46,460 --> 00:10:49,920
For one week, the soldiers would be visitors from the future.

222
00:10:49,970 --> 00:10:52,990
Strange, loud, fragile animals.

223
00:10:53,040 --> 00:10:55,030
In a world that would continue for millions

224
00:10:55,080 --> 00:10:58,260
of years without ever noticing they were gone.
